    {
      "page_title": "Variance Basics",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. What is variance?",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "==Variance== says how subtyping of `T` carries over to `Box<T>`. Given `Dog extends Animal`, does `Box<Dog>` fit `Box<Animal>`, the reverse, or neither?",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Kind",
            "Dog → Animal gives",
            "Fits"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "{green:Covariant}",
              "Box<Dog> → Box<Animal>",
              "producers, returns"
            ],
            [
              "{blue:Contravariant}",
              "Handler<Animal> → Handler<Dog>",
              "consumers, params"
            ],
            [
              "{red:Invariant}",
              "neither direction",
              "mutable read/write"
            ]
          ],
          "frame": "dashed"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. How functions behave",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Parameters are **contravariant** under `strictFunctionTypes`",
            "Return types are **covariant**",
            "Method shorthand `m(x: T)` stays ==bivariant==, even in strict mode",
            "Property form `m: (x: T) => void` is checked strictly"
          ],
          "style": "dot",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "ts",
          "lines": [
            "interface A {",
            "  m(x: Dog): void;        // loose",
            "  p: (x: Dog) => void;    // strict",
            "}"
          ],
          "note": "shorthand is bivariant, unsound"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Worked example",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "ts",
          "lines": [
            "type F<T> = (x: T) => void;",
            "let a: F<Animal> = (x) => {};",
            "let d: F<Dog> = a;     // ok",
            "let b: F<Dog> = (x) => {};",
            "let c: F<Animal> = b;  // error"
          ],
          "note": "(Animal)=>void fits (Dog)=>void only"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Assignment",
            "Allowed?",
            "Why"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "(a: Animal) => void to (d: Dog) => void",
              "{green:yes}",
              "accepts any Dog"
            ],
            [
              "(d: Dog) => void to (a: Animal) => void",
              "{red:no}",
              "may get a Cat"
            ]
          ],
          "highlightRow": 0
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "sticky",
          "title": "Unsound: arrays",
          "text": "Arrays are covariant in TS. `const a: Animal[] = dogs` is allowed, then `a.push(cat)` compiles."
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Annotation",
            "T appears in",
            "Variance"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`out T`",
              "outputs only",
              "covariant"
            ],
            [
              "`in T`",
              "inputs only",
              "contravariant"
            ],
            [
              "`in out T`",
              "both",
              "invariant"
            ]
          ],
          "frame": "dashed"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Compiler **checks** each annotation against the structure",
            "Annotations also ==speed up== assignability checks",
            "Probe: `type Check = Box<Dog> extends Box<Animal> ? 1 : 0`",
            "`1` means covariant; `0` means not covariant"
          ],
          "style": "check",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Rule of thumb",
          "text": "Only read out: `out`. Only written in: `in`. Both: `in out`, invariant."
        }
      ],
      "hero": "react",
      "subtitle": "in, out and invariant"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Variance Annotations in Code",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. Annotations and the probe",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "ts",
          "lines": [
            "interface Producer<out T> {",
            "  get(): T;",
            "}",
            "interface Consumer<in T> {",
            "  set(x: T): void;",
            "}",
            "interface Cell<in out T> {",
            "  v: T;  // read and write",
            "}"
          ],
          "note": "TS 4.7+; checked against structure"
        }
      ],
      "hero": "react",
      "subtitle": "in, out and invariant"
